WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.540
Joker​ is dividing the heck out of audiences
far and wide, including one very significant

00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:06.880
crowd: Oscar voters.

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:10.860
​The Hollywood Reporter​ recently spoke
with a number of members of the Academy of

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:15.280
Motion Picture Arts and Sciences, and like
critics and audiences, they were largely split

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:15.920
on the film.

00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:18.240
First, let's hear from the crowd that loved
it.

00:00:18.740 --> 00:00:23.540
One male member of the Members at Large Branch
who saw the film at the official Academy screening

00:00:23.540 --> 00:00:27.740
noted that the flick was well-received by
the 500 audience members, and said,

00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:28.780
"I think it's brilliant.

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:30.840
Joaquin Phoenix has never been better.

00:00:31.060 --> 00:00:35.140
[...] I thought that the [director of photography],
production designer and others created the

00:00:35.210 --> 00:00:40.440
look and feel of the period really well, and
the fact that it has very contemporary themes

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:43.240
of economic/class inequities felt just right.

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:44.860
Overall, a home run."

00:00:45.340 --> 00:00:49.960
A female member of the same branch agreed,
although she admitted that her, quote, "stomach

00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:52.340
was still churning" the day after viewing
the film.

00:00:52.660 --> 00:00:53.280
She added,

00:00:53.660 --> 00:00:57.700
"It made me uncomfortable from the very first
frame to the last, but I thought the movie

00:00:57.700 --> 00:00:58.700
was extraordinary.

00:00:58.760 --> 00:01:03.020
It's the most outstanding performance I've
seen in many years [...] It's still early,

00:01:03.020 --> 00:01:06.140
but I can certainly see myself nominating
it for best picture.

00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:10.440
And [Phoenix] has to get nominated, or the
actors branch doesn't know what it's doing."

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:14.820
A male member of the writers' branch compared
the film favorably to the last picture to

00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:18.100
score an Oscar win for an actor portraying
the Joker, saying,

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:22.480
"I thought the filmmaking was exceptional
certainly the most innovative superhero movie

00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:23.680
since The Dark Knight.

00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:28.580
[...] I haven't seen any of this season's
'Academy' movies yet, but, artistically, I

00:01:28.580 --> 00:01:32.240
think this film is stronger than many of last
year's Oscar-nominated films."

00:01:32.950 --> 00:01:36.980
Then there was one male member of the producers'
branch who boldly predicted that Joker will

00:01:36.980 --> 00:01:39.000
deliver come February, saying,

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:41.500
"I loved it and it will win Best Picture.

00:01:41.540 --> 00:01:46.439
I am really bored with all the superhero movies,
but here I really liked it because it's a

00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:48.840
great movie before it's a genre movie."

00:01:49.480 --> 00:01:53.380
To say that many of the Oscar voters who spoke
with The Hollywood Reporter were not quite

00:01:53.380 --> 00:01:55.540
so impressed would be a massive understatement.

00:01:56.220 --> 00:02:00.680
Those who disliked the film disliked it intensely,
and they didn't hold back when discussing

00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:02.980
what they perceived to be Joker's faults.

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:07.759
One female member of the executive branch,
while praising Phoenix's work, simply couldn't

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:09.440
puzzle out the point of the movie.

00:02:09.580 --> 00:02:10.080
She said,

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:11.940
"Strange, depressing film.

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:15.040
I was vaguely mesmerized, but… to what end?

00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:18.940
I found it severely lacking any specific thematic
point.

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:23.580
Great performance and impressive filmmaking,
but a rather unpleasant experience overall."

00:02:23.590 --> 00:02:28.319
One male member of the directors' branch called
the flick a "cynical mess," one that was not

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:32.819
so much influenced by the works of Martin
Scorsese as it was derivative of his works.

00:02:33.100 --> 00:02:33.700
He said,

00:02:33.820 --> 00:02:39.290
"I also found it to be more than a bit irresponsible,
in terms of its depiction of both mental illness

00:02:39.290 --> 00:02:40.060
and violence.

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:42.820
[...] It also seems the film was made only
to provoke.

00:02:43.020 --> 00:02:45.380
But I find it isn't interesting enough to
do that.

00:02:45.580 --> 00:02:48.540
Subtlety is not the director or lead actor's
strong suit here.

00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:53.340
[...] Finally, I found the film to be a big
disappointment… and one filled with self-importance."

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:57.620
A male member of the documentary branch stated
that while the film was admittedly well-made,

00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:01.970
he found it to be downright irresponsible
of the filmmakers to unleash it upon a world

00:03:01.970 --> 00:03:06.380
in which unhinged, male loners seemingly perpetrate
violence every other day.

00:03:06.520 --> 00:03:07.060
He said,

00:03:07.380 --> 00:03:11.400
"Who knows what effect it will have on some
deranged sociopath in today's climate of gun

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:11.900
violence?

00:03:12.460 --> 00:03:17.080
[...] Bottom line: technically an interestingly
experimental genre film that presents a socially

00:03:17.080 --> 00:03:20.140
dicey spectacle that I found personally repugnant."

00:03:20.920 --> 00:03:25.320
Although most Oscar voters' opinions came
down firmly on one side of the fence or the

00:03:25.320 --> 00:03:28.880
other, there was a distinct minority that
fell into the "undecided" camp.

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:33.500
One male member of the producers' branch was
befuddled as to how he should vote, even though

00:03:33.500 --> 00:03:34.960
he personally loved the film.

00:03:35.080 --> 00:03:35.820
He explained,

00:03:36.040 --> 00:03:39.080
"I don't see any reason why this movie should
be out in the universe.

00:03:39.420 --> 00:03:44.110
There is nothing in it that starts a conversation
it just pours lighter fluid on a conversation

00:03:44.110 --> 00:03:45.800
that's staring us in the face every week.

00:03:46.280 --> 00:03:49.340
[...] I don't know if it should be banned
or it should be given every award!"

00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:53.340
Another male member of the same branch was
blown away by the film's technical merits,

00:03:53.340 --> 00:03:57.960
but nevertheless found it literally too difficult
to watch due to its subject matter, saying,

00:03:58.120 --> 00:04:03.480
"I found the craft to be stunning on every
level beautiful photography, design, costumes.

00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:05.300
But I deeply despise the movie.

00:04:05.580 --> 00:04:07.290
[...] We had to drink away our discomfort.

00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:10.620
[...] It was the first movie in history that
was too dark for me."

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:15.739
Based on all of these comments, it seems like
Joker has roughly a 50/50 chance of scoring

00:04:15.740 --> 00:04:19.410
an Oscar win, and its strongest chance seems
to be in the Best Actor category.

00:04:19.720 --> 00:04:23.440
With a good number of Oscar voters on its
side, though, it's possible that the flick

00:04:23.440 --> 00:04:27.500
could score nominations for its direction
and cinematography, for which its has received

00:04:27.500 --> 00:04:28.700
pretty consistent praise.

00:04:28.920 --> 00:04:33.220
It could even buck the odds to score a Best
Picture nomination, and if it does, it's not

00:04:33.220 --> 00:04:37.560
outside the realm of possibility that it could
win; at the moment, one online odds maker

00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:42.270
has the flick dead even with Scorsese's The
Irishman as the favorites to take home the

00:04:42.270 --> 00:04:44.080
big prize, according to Bleeding Cool.

00:04:44.680 --> 00:04:49.690
Perhaps Joker really will get the last laugh
at the 91st Academy Awards ceremony, which

00:04:49.690 --> 00:04:51.960
takes place on February 9th, 2020.

00:04:52.120 --> 00:04:55.400
"Ah ha ha ha ha."

